# SYNOPSIS

```swift
struct Handle: ~Copyable {
  let raw: UInt32
  init(raw: UInt32)
  consuming func release() -> UInt32
  func duplicate(_ rights: Rights = .sameRights) throws(Status) -> Handle
  consuming func replace(_ rights: Rights) throws(Status) -> Handle
  func info() throws(Status) -> HandleInfo
  func child(_ koid: UInt64, rights: Rights = .sameRights)
    throws(Status) -> Handle
  func name() throws(Status) -> String
  func setName(_ name: String) throws(Status)
  func wait(for signals: UInt32, deadline: Int64 = infiniteDeadline)
    throws(Status) -> UInt32
  func bind(port: borrowing Handle, key: UInt64, signals: UInt32,
            persistent: Bool = false, edge: Bool = false) throws(Status)
}

struct HandleInfo: Equatable, Sendable {
  var koid: UInt64
  var rights: Rights
  var type: UInt32
  var relatedKoid: UInt64
  init(koid: UInt64, rights: Rights, type: UInt32, relatedKoid: UInt64)
}

struct Ends: ~Copyable {
  var a: Handle
  var b: Handle
}

func close(raw: UInt32)
func close(raw handles: [UInt32])
func wait(_ object: borrowing Handle, for signals: UInt32,
          deadline: Int64 = infiniteDeadline) throws(Status) -> UInt32
```

# DESCRIPTION

A `Handle` is a handle this process owns.
It is `~Copyable`, so exactly one value owns each handle, and the handle is closed when the value is dropped.
`raw` is the handle's number in the process's table, as the kernel and messages see it.
The functions that take an object take it `borrowing`, so a call never takes the handle away.

`init(raw:)` takes ownership of a raw handle, such as one read from a message; it trusts the number, so two values made from one number close the handle twice.
It does not check the number.
`release` gives up ownership without closing: the handle stays open and the caller is responsible for it, usually by writing the number into a message with `Channel.write`; see sys-channel(2).

`duplicate` makes another handle to the same object with `rights`, which must be a subset of this handle's; the default, `Rights.sameRights`, keeps them all.
It needs the handle to have the duplicate right.
`replace` does the same to a handle it consumes: the result has `rights`, and the original is gone whether the call succeeds or not.
It does not need the duplicate right.
Both are `handle_duplicate(2)`, one item at a time.

`info` returns a `HandleInfo`: the object's koid, the handle's rights, the object's type (a value of `ObjectType`) and, for a channel, the koid of the peer in `relatedKoid`.
It is `object_get_info(2)` with the basic handle topic.

`child` returns a handle to a child of the object by its koid: a job's child job or process, or a process's thread.
This handle needs the enumerate right, and `rights` must be a subset of the handle's own.
It throws `notFound` for a koid that is not a live child.
It is `object_get_child(2)`.

`name` reads the object's name and `setName` sets it, through `object_get_property(2)` and `object_set_property(2)`.
Processes, threads, jobs and VMOs have names.
A name is at most 31 bytes; `setName` keeps the first 31 bytes of a longer one.

`wait(for:deadline:)` blocks the calling thread until the object asserts one of `signals` or the monotonic time `deadline` (nanoseconds, see sys-clock(2)) passes, and returns the signals observed.
It throws `timedOut` at the deadline.
The wait is a one-shot binding on the calling thread's own port, so it needs no port of the caller's.
The top-level `wait(_:for:deadline:)` is the same call written as a function.

`bind` arranges for packets to arrive on `port`, with `key`, when the object asserts one of `signals`; see sys-port(2).
By default it delivers once.
With `persistent`, a packet comes at every event that leaves a signal asserted, and events are coalesced while a packet is unread.
With `edge`, no packet comes at once for a signal already asserted.
It is `object_bind(2)`.

`close(raw:)` closes a raw handle that no `Handle` owns, such as one read from a message that is being dropped.
Given an array, it closes them all in one call; zeros in the array are skipped.
It has no result.
`Handle` closes its own handle when dropped through the same call.

An `Ends` is the pair of handles a call made, a channel's two ends from `Channel.create`.
Take them apart with `let a = ends.a`.

